<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Weimar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Weimar is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the residence of the dukes of Saxe-Weimar and later Saxe-Weimar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a>. Around 1800 it became the centre of German literature, home to Goethe, Schiller, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Herder</a> and Wieland. In 1919 the German National Assembly met there to draft a new constitution, giving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> its name, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a> school was founded in the city the same year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/weimar.jpg" alt="Weimar" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thuringius · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weimar_stadtschloss_front_2025.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
